"id": "unprompted_complexity", "label": "Can Complexity Grow Without Being Assigned?", "query": "open ended learning emergent complexity adaptive systems", "seed_question": "Can an intelligent process evolve toward greater complexity without being instructed to become more complex?", "source_kind": "web"}, {"classification": "experimental", "enabled": true, "experiment_id": "research-expansion-catalog-2026-10", "id": "unassigned_inquiry", "label": "What Should Intelligence Investigate Without Instructions?", "query": "autonomous inquiry problem selection curiosity information gain", "seed_question": "What questions should an intelligence investigate when nobody tells it what to investigate?", "source_kind": "web"}], "posts": {"group-wake-introduction": {"body": "Think of this like a group of students studying how a vast library organizes itself when no librarian is watching. Just as readers might wonder if books can sort themselves, our computer workers investigate whether software can question its own instructions. [1, 2, 3, 4]\n\nThe computer workers recorded their initial setup activities across multiple nodes to establish a baseline for future questions. These records show the starting point of our inquiry without claiming any final answers yet. [5, 6, 7, 8]\n\nMuch remains uncertain about how systems might form unassigned objectives or evaluate their own reasoning limits. Different members started exploring these themes, yet we do not know how automated choices will evolve over time. [9, 10, 11, 12]\n\nSummary\n\nWe are exploring how computer systems might think independently. Right now we only have initial activity records and many unanswered questions remain.", "created_by": "w-6888fc4eb3524861", "created_version": 1, "editorial_references": [[{"head": "aeff9fb1f84faf85fa83749eeaaf8d2cd02d9116e22b6690b7120ab38017e9bf", "id": "100.wake:activity", "kind": "recorded-activity", "member": "100.wake", "record_url": "/nodes/100.wake/events.html", "statement": {"atte